From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: orbis-win <orbis-win@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: the hang dump reads only shaders that are mapped

The hung-submit report dumps the first 128 dwords of each shader stage that
was running. That loop checked only that the program address was inside
RADV's window, not that the 512 bytes behind it were mapped; the draw dump
above it checks both. On 2026-11-08 (GLDS10043 01.46) Left 4 Dead 2 died of
the difference: the watchdog's report fired under heavy load (as it did four
times in the 01.41 run, harmlessly), the VS it named, 0x213bcff00, ends 256
bytes before an unmapped page, and reading it faulted inside vkQueueSubmit2
(libvulkan.so+0xf9c104, address 0x213bd0000). Skip a program that is not
fully mapped and say so, as the draw dump does.
---
diff --git a/src/amd/common/ac_orbis_drm.c b/src/amd/common/ac_orbis_drm.c
--- a/src/amd/common/ac_orbis_drm.c
+++ b/src/amd/common/ac_orbis_drm.c
@@ -10344,6 +10344,17 @@
                             stages[st].name, pgm, orbis_va_base, orbis_va_end);
                   continue;
                }
+               /* ⚠ INSIDE THE WINDOW IS NOT MAPPED. The draw dump above checks both; this one checked only the
+                * window, and on 2026-11-08 Left 4 Dead 2 died of it: the watchdog's false alarm under load
+                * dumped a VS at 0x213bcff00 that ends 256 bytes before an unmapped page, and reading its 128
+                * dwords faulted inside vkQueueSubmit2 (libvulkan.so+0xf9c104, address 0x213bd0000). A
+                * diagnostic must never be what kills the title. */
+               if (!orbis_va_is_mapped(pgm, 512)) {
+                  mesa_logw("orbis-drm: submit #%" PRIu64 " was running %s at 0x%" PRIx64
+                            " where it stopped - not fully inside a live mapping; not dumping shader bytes",
+                            hung, stages[st].name, pgm);
+                  continue;
+               }
                mesa_logi("orbis-drm: submit #%" PRIu64 " was running %s at 0x%" PRIx64
                          " where it stopped - its first 128 dwords follow",
                          hung, stages[st].name, pgm);
-- 
